      "rev_n_1_ebce72_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_ebce72_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_ebce72",
        "thought_text": "Sort the array in descending order. For each element at index i (0-based), it will be the maximum in any combination of K keys that includes it and K-1 keys chosen from the remaining N-1-i smaller keys. The number of such combinations is C(N-1-i, K-1). Sum a[i] * C(N-1-i, K-1) for all valid i.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592631.8296368
      },
      "rev_n_2_24bbbc_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_24bbbc_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_24bbbc",
        "thought_text": "Use dynamic programming or combinatorics to count how many times each value contributes. Since N is up to 100,000 and K is small (50), precomputing factorials and their modular inverses allows O(1) combination calculation, making the total complexity O(N log N) due to sorting.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592631.829652
      },
      "rev_n_3_823d62_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_823d62_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_823d62",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through all possible subsets of size K, find the maximum in each, and sum them up. This brute force approach has complexity O(C(N, K) * K), which is too slow given N=100,000, so this strategy is invalid for the constraints.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592631.8296595
      },
      "rev_n_4_ce177f_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_ce177f_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_ce177f",
        "thought_text": "Sort the array in descending order. For each element at index i, it acts as the maximum in any combination of K keys formed by itself and K-1 keys chosen from the remaining N-1-i smaller elements. The contribution is a[i] * C(N-1-i, K-1). Sum these contributions modulo 10^9+7.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-7122299692391878872",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592676.997689
      },
      "rev_n_5_efe3f8_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_efe3f8_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_efe3f8",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through the sorted array and precompute binomial coefficients using Pascal's triangle or factorials with modular inverse to efficiently calculate C(n, k) for each position, ensuring O(N) or O(N log MOD) time complexity.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-7122299692391878872",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592676.9977038
      },
      "rev_n_6_6eb4e7_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_6eb4e7_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_6eb4e7",
        "thought_text": "Verify edge cases where K=1 (sum of all elements) or K > N (sum is 0), and ensure the combination formula handles cases where n < k by returning 0, preventing index errors or incorrect logic.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-7122299692391878872",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592676.9977126
      },
      "rev_n_7_fe09cd_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_fe09cd_0",
        "node_id": "n_7_fe09cd",
        "thought_text": "Sort the array in descending order. For each element at index i (0-based), it will be the maximum in any combination of K keys that includes this element and K-1 elements chosen from the remaining N-1-i smaller elements. The number of such combinations is C(N-1-i, K-1). Sum the product of each element and its combination count modulo 10^9+7.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8401082630599754757",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592699.8725562
      },
      "rev_n_8_baec81_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_8_baec81_0",
        "node_id": "n_8_baec81",
        "thought_text": "Use a Fenwick tree or Segment tree to dynamically maintain counts of available smaller elements as we iterate through the sorted array, allowing us to query the number of ways to form a group where the current element is the maximum in O(log N) time, though this is overkill compared to the direct combinatorial formula.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8401082630599754757",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592699.872572
      },
      "rev_n_9_5ea5d1_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_9_5ea5d1_0",
        "node_id": "n_9_5ea5d1",
        "thought_text": "Apply dynamic programming where dp[i][j] represents the sum of maximums for combinations of size j using the first i sorted elements. Transition by either including the i-th element as the max or not. This would be O(N*K), which is acceptable given K<=50, but less efficient than the O(N log N) sorting approach.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8401082630599754757",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592699.87258
      },
      "rev_n_10_50b846_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_10_50b846_0",
        "node_id": "n_10_50b846",
        "thought_text": "Sort the array in descending order. For each element at index i, it acts as the maximum in any combination of K keys formed by itself and K-1 keys chosen from the remaining N-1-i smaller elements. The contribution is a[i] * C(N-1-i, K-1). Sum these contributions modulo 10^9+7.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-6360526066877120223",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592767.2417536
      },
      "rev_n_11_507c1b_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_11_507c1b_0",
        "node_id": "n_11_507c1b",
        "thought_text": "Sort the array in ascending order. Iterate from the largest element backwards. For an element at index i (0-based from start), it is the maximum if we pick it and K-1 elements from the indices greater than i. The count is C(i, K-1). Sum a[i] * C(i, K-1) for valid i.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-6360526066877120223",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592767.2417707
      },
      "rev_n_12_3fc8a2_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_12_3fc8a2_0",
        "node_id": "n_12_3fc8a2",
        "thought_text": "Use dynamic programming to count combinations. Let dp[j] be the number of ways to choose j items from the suffix. Update dp table as we iterate through sorted keys, multiplying the current key's value by the number of ways to complete a set of size K where this key is the maximum.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-6360526066877120223",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592767.2417781
      },
      "rev_n_13_18b376_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_13_18b376_0",
        "node_id": "n_13_18b376",
        "thought_text": "Sort the array in descending order and iterate through each element. For an element at index i, it acts as the maximum in any subset of size K formed by itself and K-1 elements chosen from the remaining N-1-i smaller elements. The contribution is a[i] * C(N-1-i, K-1). Sum these contributions modulo 10^9+7.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "4676979928561812370",
