      "rev_n_14_c07b94_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_14_c07b94_0",
        "node_id": "n_14_c07b94",
        "thought_text": "Use a Fenwick tree (Binary Indexed Tree) or Segment Tree to dynamically track the count of available smaller elements as we process the array, allowing us to calculate combinations on the fly without pre-sorting if the input order matters for some variant, though sorting is still optimal here.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "4676979928561812370",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592783.7768652
      },
      "rev_n_15_598b60_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_15_598b60_0",
        "node_id": "n_15_598b60",
        "thought_text": "Apply a sliding window approach combined with Lucas theorem if N were extremely large, but since N is 100,000 and K is small, standard precomputed factorials are superior; this strategy is over-engineering for the given constraints.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "4676979928561812370",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592783.7768738
      },
      "rev_n_16_cc1a63_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_16_cc1a63_0",
        "node_id": "n_16_cc1a63",
        "thought_text": "Sort the array in descending order. For each element at index i, it acts as the maximum in combinations formed by itself and K-1 elements chosen from the remaining N-1-i smaller elements. The contribution is a[i] * C(N-1-i, K-1). Sum these contributions modulo 10^9+7.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "1466911556228452205",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592801.8658824
      },
      "rev_n_17_07ea06_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_17_07ea06_0",
        "node_id": "n_17_07ea06",
        "thought_text": "Use dynamic programming to compute the sum directly without explicit binomial coefficient formulas. Define dp[i][j] as the sum of maximums for choosing j keys from the first i sorted keys. Transition by considering whether the i-th key is the maximum or not. This avoids precomputing factorials and modular inverses.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "1466911556228452205",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592801.865898
      },
      "rev_n_18_f91100_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_18_f91100_0",
        "node_id": "n_18_f91100",
        "thought_text": "Since K is very small (<= 50), iterate through all possible subsets of size K using recursion or bit manipulation, find the maximum in each subset, and accumulate the sum. Although O(C(N, K)) is too slow for N=100,000, this strategy is conceptually simple and might work if N was smaller, serving as a baseline verification method.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "1466911556228452205",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592801.8659065
      },
      "rev_n_19_70915f_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_19_70915f_0",
        "node_id": "n_19_70915f",
        "thought_text": "Sort the array in descending order. For each element at index i, it acts as the maximum in combinations formed by itself and K-1 elements chosen from the remaining N-1-i smaller elements. The contribution is a[i] * C(N-1-i, K-1). Sum these contributions modulo 10^9+7.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8567172741293311838",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592832.3843489
      },
      "rev_n_20_745cb3_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_20_745cb3_0",
        "node_id": "n_20_745cb3",
        "thought_text": "Precompute factorials and inverse factorials to efficiently calculate combinations C(n, k) in O(1) time, ensuring the overall complexity is dominated by sorting O(N log N), which fits within the N=100,000 constraint.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8567172741293311838",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592832.3843648
      },
      "rev_n_21_e3ab46_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_21_e3ab46_0",
        "node_id": "n_21_e3ab46",
        "thought_text": "Handle edge cases explicitly: if K=1, the answer is the sum of all elements; if K > N, the answer is 0. Ensure the combination function returns 0 when n < k to avoid logical errors during iteration.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8567172741293311838",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592832.3843737
      },
      "rev_n_22_014ae1_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_22_014ae1_0",
        "node_id": "n_22_014ae1",
        "thought_text": "Sort the array in ascending order. For each element at index i (0-based), if it is the maximum of a chosen subset of size K, it must be paired with exactly (i choose K-1) other elements from the preceding i elements. Sum the contribution of each element as (value * combinations) modulo 10^9+7.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "2936645758865112891",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592850.3484664
      },
      "rev_n_23_f47f89_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_23_f47f89_0",
        "node_id": "n_23_f47f89",
        "thought_text": "Use a sliding window or prefix sum approach combined with combinatorics. Since K is small, we can iterate through sorted elements and maintain the count of valid subsets where the current element is the maximum, updating the total sum incrementally to avoid recalculating large combinations repeatedly.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "2936645758865112891",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592850.3484821
      },
      "rev_n_24_3e9046_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_24_3e9046_0",
        "node_id": "n_24_3e9046",
        "thought_text": "Apply dynamic programming where dp[j] stores the number of ways to form a subset of size j using a subset of the processed elements. Update the DP table for each new element by considering it as the new maximum, shifting counts from dp[j-1] to the current contribution. This avoids explicit combination formulas but has higher constant overhead.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "2936645758865112891",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794592850.3484907
      }
